Hi,

This is not really a bug but a feature of numerical optimization. Try decreasing the convergence criterion from 1e-10 to 1e-11 as follows:

mod_fit <- sem(sample.cov = sample_cov,

               sample.nobs = 969,

               model = mod1,

               control = list(rel.tol = 1e-11),

               verbose = TRUE)

The error message “nlminb message says:  singular convergence (7)” tells you that you are trying to estimate more parameters than what your data can support. Singular convergence means (as far as I know) that the Hessian matrix that contains the second derivatives of the likelihood function has become singular. This means that the model is not identified.

So why do you not get an error message using the original tolerance? The reason is that the tolerance means how close to maximum you need to get for the computer to declare convergence. If your tolerance is too high, the computer will declare convergence before it gets to the point in the parameter space where Hessian becomes singular.

Mikko

