Date: Sun, 29 May 2022 21:33:05 +0000
Subject: Variability in residual variance z statistics in lavaan output
Message-ID: <J2zHZ28MC4k.mxpMEaeOBwAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/J2zHZ28MC4k/m/mxpMEaeOBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I have a script that creates a three-factor model with the same pattern of loadings and residual variances and factor correlations for all three factors. But the reported z statistics for the residual variance estimates seem odd:

Variances:
                   Estimate  Std.Err  z-value  P(>|z|)
   .y1                0.360    0.005   71.724    0.000
   .y2                0.510    0.005  106.845    0.000
   .y3                0.640    0.005  130.588    0.000
   .y4                0.360    0.005   78.341    0.000
   .y5                0.510    0.005  112.299    0.000
   .y6                0.640    0.005  132.904    0.000
   .y7                0.360    0.005   71.724    0.000
   .y8                0.510    0.005  106.845    0.000
   .y9                0.640    0.005  130.588    0.000
    f1                1.000
    f2                1.000
    f3                1.000
I would expect to see the same three z statistics repeated for all three factors' indicators--just as the estimates are repeated precisely--but the z values for the second factor's indicators are different from those for the first and third factors' indicators, even though the three factors are identical in every way. This analysis creates a sample of 50,000 multinormal observations, and maybe that sample size is magnifying something--but that something still shouldn't be there.

Here is the script--

# Create covariance matrix of common factors, Phi
# and covariance matrix of specific factors, Theta

Phi<-matrix(data=c(1,.5,.25,.5,1,.5,.25,.5,1),nrow=3,byrow=T)
Theta<-diag(x=c(.36,.51,.64,.36,.51,.64,.36,.51,.64))

# Create one block diagonal matrix combining Phi and Theta
# so that we can specify that they are orthogonal

library(Matrix)
bigcov<-bdiag(Phi,Theta)

# Simulate 50,000 observations on common and specific factors

library(MASS)
bigdat<-mvrnorm(n=50000,mu=c(rep(0,12)),Sigma=bigcov)

# Apply Bollen-Stine adjustment to remove sampling variance
# from data on common and specific factors

library(expm)
bigdat2<-(bigdat-mean(bigdat))%*%solve(sqrtm(cov(bigdat)))%*%sqrtm(bigcov)

# Create values on observed variables y1 - y8

colnames(bigdat2)<-c(paste("f",1:3,sep=""),paste("e",1:9,sep=""))
bigdat2<-as.data.frame(bigdat2)
y1<-0.8*bigdat2$f1+bigdat2$e1
y2<-0.7*bigdat2$f1+bigdat2$e2
y3<-0.6*bigdat2$f1+bigdat2$e3
y4<-0.8*bigdat2$f2+bigdat2$e4
y5<-0.7*bigdat2$f2+bigdat2$e5
y6<-0.6*bigdat2$f2+bigdat2$e6
y7<-0.8*bigdat2$f3+bigdat2$e7
y8<-0.7*bigdat2$f3+bigdat2$e8
y9<-0.6*bigdat2$f3+bigdat2$e9
bigovdat<-cbind(y1,y2,y3,y4,y5,y6,y7,y8,y9)

# Use lavaan syntax to specify lavaan model

model1a<-'f1=~y1+y2+y3
        f2=~y4+y5+y6
        f3=~y7+y8+y9
        f1~~f2
        f2~~f3
        f1~~f3'

# Estimate factor model with standardized common factors

library(lavaan)
bigout<-sem(model=model1a,data=bigovdat,auto.fix.first=F,std.lv <http://std.lv>=T)
summary(bigout)

--Ed Rigdon

